CLOSURE_CHECKS_CLASSIFIER_AUDIT: {
  "schema_version": 1,
  "migration_run_id": "99fd3d04-aaa0-410b-bf82-e80bff09976c",
  "snapshot_timestamp": "2026-05-22T01:22:19Z",
  "ticket_id": "c6de-76e7-5e4b-4968",
  "items": [
    {
      "original_text": "New `plugins/dso/scripts/dso_ci_review/` Python package replaces bash orchestration. Modules: `runner.py` (entry-point), `classifier.py` (tier selection \u2014 pure function), `dispatch.py` (`asyncio.gather` for parallel specialists + overlay calls), `providers/` (LiteLLM-backed clients behind a common interface), `findings.py` (single JSON normalization implementation replacing the 5+ inline heredocs). `ci-llm-review-runner.sh` reduced to \u226450 lines and exists only as a thin shim that resolves `_PLUGIN_ROOT` and execs `python3 -m dso_ci_review.runner`. `llm-api-call.sh` deleted. References in `CONFIGURATION-REFERENCE.md`, `ci-overlay-flags.md`, and `onboarding/SKILL.md` updated to reflect the new module layout.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes a durable architectural state where bash orchestration is replaced by a Python package with specific modules and reduced shell scripts, not a one-time completion task"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "LiteLLM-backed provider abstraction: Anthropic and OpenAI both functional in CI runs, verified by an integration test exercising each provider end-to-end against fixture diffs. LiteLLM is pinned to an exact version (`litellm==<version>`) in the package's lockfile \u2014 compatible-release (`~=`) and range pinning (`>=`) are explicitly rejected by a CI lint step that scans the lockfile, to remain immune to the supply-chain attack class confirmed in March 2026 (versions 1.82.7 / 1.82.8). A parity-test gate is required before any LiteLLM version bump is accepted. Adding the Gemini follow-on epic requires only a new entry under `providers/` plus a config key \u2014 no changes to `runner.py`, `dispatch.py`, or `findings.py`. Verified by adding a stub `mock` provider in tests as the third implementation.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 5,
        "rationale": "describes durable system capabilities (LiteLLM abstraction, provider interchangeability, supply-chain protections) that persist after completion"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Schema-equivalent `reviewer-findings.json` parity across the fixture corpus: same fixture diffs produce findings JSON that conforms to the existing schema and preserves the full set of pre-migration `critical` / `important` / `fragile` findings (additive findings allowed but no critical / important / fragile finding may be dropped). The parity test additionally asserts zero dropped findings relative to the pre-migration baseline on the fixture corpus \u2014 eliminating the silent-drop failure mode of the current bash regex-fallback JSON parsing. Parity-test job runs in CI for the duration of the epic and is removed at epic close. Pre-migration baseline captured as a checked-in golden corpus in story 1, before migration begins, and reused by the downstream tool-use epic's before/after grounded-finding comparison.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "uncertain",
        "ranking": 2,
        "rationale": "mixes durable end-state (schema parity, finding preservation) with transitional elements (test job removal at epic close, baseline capture timing)"
      },
      "user_decision": "accept",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "`asyncio.gather` partial-failure handling produces a user-observable improvement vs. the current bash behavior: when one specialist or overlay provider call fails with **any LiteLLM exception** (including but not limited to 5xx-class, network failure, timeout, rate-limit/quota, authentication, and content-policy errors), the review degrades to partial findings instead of aborting the whole run, and the engineer sees an explicit error entry for the failed specialist on the PR (current bash runner aborts the entire review on a single error and produces invalid JSON in slot files when the provider returns an error envelope that the regex-fallback parser cannot extract findings from). **Slot files always contain either valid findings JSON or a structured error entry conforming to the findings schema; raw provider error response text is never written to a slot file.** Verified by a test that injects representative exceptions from each LiteLLM exception class (`RateLimitError`, `BadRequestError`, `AuthenticationError`, `ContentPolicyViolationError`, `InternalServerError`, `Timeout`) into one specialist call and asserts the resulting `reviewer-findings.json` includes (a) the surviving specialists' findings and (b) an explicit error entry for the failed specialist that surfaces in the PR review summary; in particular, the slot file passes JSON schema validation in every error case.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 5,
        "rationale": "describes durable system behavior (graceful partial-failure handling with valid output) that persists after this work, not a one-time completion task"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Per-module unit tests using `respx` / `httpx.MockTransport`-style provider mocks (no `PATH`-mocked curl in unit tests). Integration tests exercise both Anthropic and OpenAI end-to-end. Story 1 transport-verification acceptance criterion: instantiate a LiteLLM client in the same configuration `providers/` will use and assert its internal HTTP transport is `httpx`-based for the Anthropic and OpenAI provider paths. If `aiohttp` is detected on any path, substitute `pytest-httpx` for `respx` as the test mock \u2014 do not rely on `DISABLE_AIOHTTP_TRANSPORT` or the `httpx_client` parameter as a forcing mechanism, as both have open reliability bugs across providers (LiteLLM #12581, #13049). Document the detected transport per provider before any provider unit tests are written. CI install pipeline uses `actions/setup-python` (or platform equivalent) with `cache: 'pip'` against a checked-in lockfile so cold-runner pip install does not regress CI run duration. CI run duration neutral-or-better: post-migration p50 and p95 CI review-job duration on the fixture corpus is no greater than the bash-baseline duration captured before migration begins; verified by comparison script at epic close. All existing CI invocation surfaces (`ci-llm-review-runner.sh` shim, `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `templates/ci-dso-staged.yml`, `tests/scripts/test-ci-llm-review-job.sh`, `test-ci-llm-review-runner.sh`) continue to work without per-platform conditionals.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "uncertain",
        "ranking": 2,
        "rationale": "mixes durable end-state properties (per-module unit tests using respx, documented transport detection, CI cache setup) with transitional tasks (migration completion, transport substitution decisions, CI duration verification) making it ambiguous whether the item targets ongoing system behavior or a one-time migration milestone"
      },
      "user_decision": "accept",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "Provider chain + tier-upgrade fallback via LiteLLM, with `fallback_exhausted` signal. Every `litellm.completion(...)` call in `dso_ci_review/dispatch.py` is constructed with `streaming=False` (avoiding LiteLLM streaming-mode fallback bugs #22296, #6532) and: `fallbacks=[...]` rendered from `dso-config.conf` key `ci_review.provider_chain` (ordered provider list, e.g., `[anthropic, openai]`) \u00d7 the agent's declared `model:` field expressed against each non-primary provider \u2014 gives cross-provider fallback on any LiteLLM exception; `context_window_fallbacks=[...]` rendered from the agent's `model:` field upgraded through `[haiku, sonnet, opus]` on the agent's primary provider until the top of the ladder \u2014 gives same-provider tier upgrade on `ContextWindowExceededError`. Agents already at `opus` (deep arch, overlay reviewers) get an empty list (no upgrade available); the opus terminal case for `ContextWindowExceededError` is documented operator-facing as falling through to `fallback_exhausted` rather than escalating cross-provider. When LiteLLM exhausts both lists, the dispatcher catches the terminal `litellm.exceptions.OpenAIError`-class exception and (1) writes a `fallback_exhausted` entry into `reviewer-findings.json` (atomic write via temp-file + rename; per-agent file ownership documented to prevent parallel-write corruption when deep tier dispatches 3 specialists + arch synthesis) conforming to a documented schema (`docs/contracts/ci-review-fallback-exhausted.md`) with fields: `agent_id`, `primary_model`, `attempted_cross_provider[]`, `attempted_context_models[]`, `final_exception_class`, `final_exception_message` \u2014 schema is the consumption contract for the downstream Copilot-reviewer-gate epic; (2) renders any successful fallback hops as one-line notes in the PR review summary in stable format `[fallback: <agent> <primary_model> -> <fallback_model> (<exception_class>)]`. Startup precondition: dispatcher refuses to run with a clear error when any provider in `ci_review.provider_chain` lacks credentials in the runtime environment, so credential drift between local config and CI does not silently mask rate-limit failures as auth errors in `final_exception_class`. `provider_chain` parsing is strict: lowercase canonical LiteLLM provider names only; whitespace trimmed; unknown providers reject at config-load time. A test asserts `streaming=False` is preserved through every fallback hop (defending against LiteLLM router defaults or per-entry `stream: true` overrides). Verified by: (a) unit test injecting `RateLimitError` from primary, asserting next provider answers, hop rendered, review completes; (b) unit test injecting `ContextWindowExceededError` from primary tier-model, asserting next tier-model on same provider answers, hop rendered, review completes; (c) unit test injecting failure on every chain entry, asserting `fallback_exhausted` JSON entry written with all six fields populated and surviving agents' partial findings preserved per SC4; (d) contract conformance test loading the schema against the JSON; (e) test asserting `streaming=False` preservation across hops; (f) test asserting startup-time rejection when a chained provider's credential is absent.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes a durable system capability (fallback routing with tier escalation and signal emission) that persists once implemented, phrased as ongoing dispatcher behavior across completion calls"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    }
  ]
}
